{
	title: 'Quirks: one advertised call syntax, leniency in one module',
	spec: 'Prompts, HELP and examples teach one obvious syntax per tool. All leniency for call text lives in src/host/quirks.ts, apart from the strict parser and the tools. native-tool resolves call text strictly first; only a strict failure goes to quirks, which try their rewrites in order and resolve again. The first rewrite that resolves wins; if none does, the strict error is reported unchanged. A quirk accepts only input with one possible meaning; ambiguous input fails with the canonical syntax. Quirks apply to every model and to humans typing calls in hal/tool. Each quirk names the case it accepts and who was seen writing it; every firing goes to state/diag.log with quirk, model and session, so unused quirks can be deleted. Existing leniency moves in: extras/edit-quirks.ts (removed; its per-model lists dropped), native-tool\'s [{ ... }] unwrap and READ_BLOB alias, SyntaxSpec.aliases field renames, and COMPACT\'s unquoted summary. New quirk, rest of line: a tool may declare a main text field; when its call fails strictly, the rest of the first line after any earlier positional arguments becomes that field verbatim, quotes and backslashes kept (bash ls -l; bash echo "a b" | wc; send 12-abc hello there). A trailing { ... } counts as options only if it parses and names only that tool\'s fields; otherwise it is part of the text. Tools whose several words mean several values (READ paths) keep their strict meaning.',
	status: 'planned',
